ou to slap on a battery  and another chip full of ram  and that battery just gives you a little  trickle of electricity that keeps the  contents of the ram alive even when it's  not in your system so now you've got an  extra eight kilobytes that you can just  write whatever you want to  which leads us to another problem  um you start seeing this like in game  manuals or in actual games themselves um  you'll see things where it's like hold  in the reset button as you turn the  power off very important you will  destroy your game if you don't do this  what is going on with this well  uh it turns out when you turn off the  power to your nes it doesn't just go  from on to off immediately  what actually happens is you turn off  the power and the processor still has a  little bit of residual electricity in it  and that just  fades away  relatively quickly but it's enough time  that like the processor is still running  and as the processor has less and less  electricity available to it it starts  acting more and more erratic  so it's unlikely  but it is possible that as you turn off  your system that processor is going to  do something totally unpredictable and  write data into that save ram and just  corrupt everything that you've put there  so you start seeing these warnings  because holding the reset button puts  that processor into a very tight little  loop where it can't break out and do  something else  but if you're a game developer you don't  know  that your player actually did this  you don't know  if the ram has been messed with or not  so  how do you do that because you want to  load the save data but you also don't  want to load something that's been  turned into garbage  um  dragon warrior the us release of dragon  quest 1 did a really interesting thing  where they just write the save file many  many times so i think they make  something like eight copies of your save  into that save ram and they write a  little checksum with each of those  so when dragon warrior goes to load your  save game it reads the first one it  checks the checksum and if they match  cool it will load your game  if they don't it just goes to the next  one and the next one and the next one  and if it goes through all of them and  all of them are bad then it just shows  you like the standard title screen that  says start a new game  but this is how it gets around that like  did you hold the reset button or not  problem  so i've been up here talking to you for  about half an hour now  you may be wondering what is the point  of all of this why are you rambling at  us about these old games  um  there are a number of reasons but uh i  think the one that really kind of sticks  out to me is  doing all this research learning all  these things about all these old games  has shown me that there is value in  embracing the stupid  uh what i mean by that is  you can have a problem that you need to  solve and you can come up with a really  dumb solution to it that does what you  need to do  and that's fine there is nothing wrong  with that  eventually you might find a situation  that proves to you that your situation  is too dumb and it doesn't solve  everything you need to do and at that  point you can go back and you can change  it but up to that point  just get things done ship them out the  door who cares um  i would rather  hard code a table of 256 random numbers  and ship final fantasy  then sit there for months trying to  figure out the perfect random number  generating algorithm  that's my takeaway you may have your own  that's the bulk of what i've got if this  is something that you are interested in  if you want to know more about how the  nes works developing for it anything  like that uh i'm going to show you some  resources uh one i will definitely point  to is nathan altus's book i am error  from 2015.  this is part of an mit press book series  called platform studies the whole series  is great this book in particular goes  into the design decisions