at uh from startup when  you first hit that power button that's  how it seeds that number  you can um i simplified a little bit  there um but as far as like the nes  hardware is concerned it just knows  there are two pattern tables one is  sprites one is backgrounds  chips like that mmc one that enables you  to do saving let you put in more than  eight kilobytes of graphics data you can  only expose eight kilobytes to the  system at a time but you can like change  which eight kilobytes it's seeing  um there are also later chips that  enabled you to set up instead of chr rom  where it's like baked in you could do  chr ram where you could literally just  write in real time what goes into those  pattern tables  um there are games like battle toads or  metal storm that use that to do these  really complicated parallax effects  because they're constantly redrawing  what is in the tiles while you're  playing  you can to some degree um there is a  decently sized community of people who  use c to write nes games there is  basically a c library that  you use the functions in the c library  and then it can compile down into 6502  assembly  there's a guy named douglas freaker i  believe who  has like a whole tutorial series on c  development for the nes  i i heard about that one recently i have  not had a chance to poke around uh i  don't think i am the person to give a  talk about that  but it sounds super cool yeah  someone asked how does the game genie  work  the game genie literally just kind of  intercepts in between the cartridge and  the system and it allows you to like  overwrite ram values so like the  cartridge code will say hey do this to  the ram inside the nes and the game  genie will intercept that message and  actually set it to something else  instead  game genie has its own complicated  password system of how the password you  type in turns into like what to do to a  particular ram address  how long until the ram battery dies uh  really depends i'm not sure um